.Dd July 6, 2026
.Dt XTC_FREE 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_free ,
.Nm xtc_malloc ,
.Nm xtc_calloc ,
.Nm xtc_realloc ,
.Nm xtc_aligned_alloc ,
.Nm xtc_aligned_free ,
.Nm xtc_clock_mono ,
.Nm xtc_clock_real ,
.Nm xtc_sleep_ns ,
.Nm xtc_atomic_i64_load ,
.Nm xtc_atomic_i64_add
.Nd public allocation, clock, sleep, and atomic helpers
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_free "void *p"
.Ft void *
.Fn xtc_malloc "size_t size"
.Ft void *
.Fn xtc_calloc "size_t n" "size_t size"
.Ft void *
.Fn xtc_realloc "void *p" "size_t size"
.Ft void *
.Fn xtc_aligned_alloc "size_t align" "size_t size"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_aligned_free "void *p"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn xtc_clock_mono "void"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn xtc_clock_real "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_sleep_ns "int64_t ns"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_load "const int64_t *p"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_add "int64_t *p" "int64_t delta"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These are the public complement to the library's internal portability
layer: a CONSUMER of libxtc uses only these
.Fn xtc_* No entry points and never the internal Fn __os_* No wrappers.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_free
releases a heap buffer that a libxtc call allocated and handed to the
caller to own.
Several APIs return caller-owned buffers and document that they must be
released with
.Fn xtc_free :
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the message buffer from
.Xr xtc_recv 3 ,
.Fn xtc_recv_match ,
and
.Fn xtc_recv_correlate ;
.It
the frame from
.Fn xtc_net_recv_frame ;
.It
the reply buffer from
.Fn xtc_osproc_call
and
.Fn xtc_svr_call .
.El
.Pp
These buffers are allocated by libxtc's own allocator, which is not
necessarily the C library
.Xr malloc 3 Ns / Ns Xr free 3 ,
so they must be released through
.Fn xtc_free
rather than plain
.Xr free 3 .
Passing
.Dv NULL
is a no-op.
.Fn xtc_free
is safe to call from any thread.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_malloc , Fn xtc_calloc , No and Fn xtc_realloc No allocate through
libxtc's allocator (the same one
.Fn xtc_free
releases, overridable via the allocator hook), returning the pointer
directly and
.Dv NULL
on failure; the result is released with
.Fn xtc_free .
A zero size yields a unique freeable pointer, not
.Dv NULL .
.Fn xtc_aligned_alloc
returns memory aligned to
.Fa align
(a power of two), released ONLY with
.Fn xtc_aligned_free
(never
.Fn xtc_free ) .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_clock_mono
and
.Fn xtc_clock_real
read the monotonic and real clocks in nanoseconds (returning 0 on the
rare query failure).
.Fn xtc_sleep_ns
sleeps the calling OS THREAD; inside a fiber use
.Xr xtc_proc_sleep 3
instead, which parks the fiber without blocking the loop.
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_load
and
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_add
(which returns the prior value) are the minimal public atomics for a
shared 64-bit counter.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_free
and
.Fn xtc_aligned_free
return no value.
.Fn xtc_malloc , Fn xtc_calloc , Fn xtc_realloc , No and
.Fn xtc_aligned_alloc
return the allocated pointer, or
.Dv NULL
on failure.
.Fn xtc_clock_mono No and Fn xtc_clock_real No return a nanosecond count
(0 on failure).
.Fn xtc_sleep_ns
returns
.Dv XTC_OK
or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code.
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_load
returns the current value;
.Fn xtc_atomic_i64_add
returns the value before the add.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_net 3 ,
.Xr xtc_osproc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_svr 3
